# Record 160 checkpoint.
talk(160,'1968-11-04','A. A. Kirillov','P000257-P000261','Characters, coadjoint orbits, and Plancherel measure',r'The actual talk occupies P000257--P000260. It moves from distribution characters and the affine group to symplectic coadjoint orbits, orbital Fourier transforms, integrality, the compact rank-one example, and Plancherel measure. A Pfaffian exercise and brief remarks on matrix products, tensor products, and Rodrigues formulas close the page. The unrestricted all-group wording is separated from the nilpotent and compact theorems actually established in \citet{kirillov1962unitary,kirillov1968characters}.')
item(160,'P000257','definition','Distribution characters and their test-function domain',r'\(T(\varphi)=\int_G\varphi(g)T(g)\,dg\); if its trace exists, \(\operatorname{tr}T(\varphi)=\langle\chi,\varphi\rangle\). The examples appear no worse than measures, but no general theorem is asserted.',r'For a strongly continuous unitary representation \(\pi\) of a Lie group with a fixed left Haar measure, \(\pi(\varphi)\) is bounded for \(\varphi\in C_c^\infty(G)\). A distribution character on this test space is defined when these operators are trace class and \(\varphi\mapsto\operatorname{Tr}\pi(\varphi)\) is distribution-continuous. These conditions are additional assumptions; the trace is not defined for an arbitrary irreducible representation of an arbitrary Lie group.',r'The trace formulation and its need for hypotheses are the starting point of \citet[Introduction and \S2]{kirillov1968characters}.',['kirillov1968characters'],match='partial',reconstruction='The continuity required for a distribution, the Haar choice, and the distinction between a bounded and trace-class operator are made explicit.')
item(160,'P000257','remark','The affine group and a restricted test-function ideal',r'For \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), the test functions must satisfy \(\int\varphi(a,b)\,db=0\) for every \(a\); the preceding sentence says examples have no trace for any \(\varphi\).',r'For the connected affine group \(a>0\), its infinite-dimensional irreducibles are realized on the positive or negative half-line in the dual of the translation subgroup. The condition that the Fourier transform in \(b\) vanish at frequency zero removes the endpoint obstruction to a trace. The literal claim that no test function has a trace is too strong: the zero test function always does, and restricted ideals can contain nonzero examples.',r'The relevant reduction through a commutative normal subgroup is discussed in \citet[\S2]{kirillov1968characters}; this does not certify an unrestricted character on all test functions.',['kirillov1968characters'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The half-line realization explains the recorded cancellation condition; the notebook does not state a complete necessary-and-sufficient trace-class criterion.')
item(160,'P000258','construction','The symplectic form on a coadjoint orbit',r'\(0\to\mathcal H\to\mathfrak g\to T_F\Omega\to0\); \(B_F(\xi,\eta)=(2\pi)^{-1}\langle F,[X,Y]\rangle\), independent of lifts.',r'For a coadjoint orbit \(\Omega\subset\mathfrak g^*\), the stabilizer Lie algebra is \(\mathfrak g_F=\{X:\langle F,[X,Y]\rangle=0\text{ for all }Y\}\). The displayed bilinear form descends to \(\mathfrak g/\mathfrak g_F\), is nondegenerate, is invariant under the coadjoint action, and is closed by the Jacobi identity. A consistent sign convention for fundamental vector fields is understood.',r'\citet[\S1, Lemmas~1--3]{kirillov1968characters} proves these statements; the normalization agrees with the notebook.',['kirillov1968characters'],literal='correct')
item(160,'P000258','construction','Liouville measure and the orbital Fourier distribution',r'\(\beta=e^B=1+B+\cdots+B^k/k!\), with only its top term integrated; \(\mathcal J_\Omega(X)=\int_\Omega e^{i\langle F,X\rangle}\beta(F)\).',r'On a \(2k\)-dimensional symplectic coadjoint orbit, \(B^k/k!\) is an invariant positive density after the symplectic orientation is chosen. Its Fourier transform is interpreted distributionally whenever integration against the relevant Fourier transforms converges; a noncompact orbital integral need not be an absolutely convergent function of \(X\).',r'This is the measure used in the orbit formulas of \citet[\S1]{kirillov1968characters}.',['kirillov1968characters'],reconstruction='The top-degree component, orientation, and distributional meaning are specified; a growth condition must precede a tempered-distribution claim.')
item(160,'P000258-P000259','claim','The unqualified universal orbital character formula',r'The assertion \(\chi(\exp X)=\mathcal J_\Omega(X)\) is marked false; it is replaced, for every irreducible representation, by \(\chi(\exp X)=p_\Omega(X)^{-1}\mathcal J_\Omega(X)\), \(p_\Omega(0)=1\), smooth.',r'A precise local character formula must specify a class of groups and representations, the orbit or cycle attached to each representation, a test-function domain, the exponential-chart Jacobian, and whether the factor is smooth and nonvanishing on that chart. Those data are not specified for the all-group assertion. The nilpotent and compact instances below are precise; they do not imply this universal statement.',r'\citet[Introduction, \S\S2--3]{kirillov1968characters} proves selected cases and reductions, not this all-group assertion. The already noted affine trace obstruction prevents treating the displayed expression as a distribution identity on an unstated common test space.',['kirillov1968characters'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',reconstruction='No globally defined quotient of distributions is asserted: such a quotient is not an operation in distribution theory.')
item(160,'P000259','theorem','The simply connected nilpotent orbit formula',r'\(p_\Omega=1\) for nilpotent groups; orbits do correspond to representations in that case.',r'For a connected simply connected nilpotent real Lie group, equivalence classes of irreducible unitary representations are parametrized by coadjoint orbits. With compatible Haar, Fourier, and symplectic normalizations, the distribution character pulled back by the global exponential diffeomorphism is the Fourier transform of the orbit Liouville measure; the exponential Jacobian is one.',r'This is pre-existing: \citet[Theorems~5.1--5.2 and~7.4]{kirillov1962unitary}, received 17 October 1961 and published in 1962.',['kirillov1962unitary'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(160,'P000259','theorem','Compact characters require the rho-shifted regular orbit',r'For an arbitrary compact group all orbits are said to be in general position; \(p_\Omega=\sqrt\rho\) is suggested.',r'For a compact connected semisimple group and an irreducible representation of dominant integral highest weight \(\lambda\), the character formula uses the regular coadjoint orbit through \(\lambda+\rho\), where \(\rho\) is half the sum of positive roots. Near zero, the correction is the analytic square root \(j(X)^{1/2}\) of \(j(X)=\det_{\mathfrak g}((1-e^{-\operatorname{ad}X})/\operatorname{ad}X)\). Singular coadjoint orbits certainly exist; the zero orbit is one.',r'The precise compact result is \citet[\S3, Theorem~2]{kirillov1968characters}; its coordinates \(f_k\) and highest weights \(f_k-1\) exhibit the shift.',['kirillov1968characters'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The word all is restricted to the regular shifted orbits attached by this character formula, not to every orbit in the dual.')
item(160,'P000259','conjecture','The proposed product of modified hyperbolic sines',r'\(\operatorname{sth}x=\sinh(x/2)/(x/2)\); \(p_\Omega(X)=\prod_\alpha\operatorname{sth}\langle\alpha,X\rangle\) is marked conjecture, with \(\alpha\) called roots.',r'For a compact connected semisimple group, on a Cartan subalgebra and with complexified roots, \(j(X)^{1/2}=\prod_{\alpha>0}\sinh(\alpha(X)/2)/(\alpha(X)/2)\), normalized to one at zero. The product is over positive roots; taking both signs squares it. Outside that setting the notebook does not identify a root multiset depending on \(\Omega\), or the intended generality of the conjecture.',r'The compact factor is already present in \citet[\S3]{kirillov1968characters}. No exact general root-product theorem was identified from this notation; the general extension is therefore not entered as a newly solved or open problem.',['kirillov1968characters'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The precise positive-root formula is supplied as a verified special case; it is not asserted to recover the full conjecture.')
item(160,'P000259','claim','Integrality and characters of the orbit fundamental group',r'Choose \(\Omega\) with \([B]\in H^2(\Omega,\mathbb Z)\); it corresponds to as many representations as characters of \(\pi_1(\Omega)\), which is said to be commutative.',r'For a connected symplectic manifold, an integral class \([B]\) is the condition for a Hermitian line bundle with a unitary connection of curvature \(2\pi iB\). When one such connection exists, isomorphism classes with this curvature form a torsor for flat unitary line bundles, classified by \(\operatorname{Hom}(\pi_1(\Omega),U(1))\). This geometric statement neither requires \(\pi_1\) itself to be abelian nor provides, by itself, a bijection with the unitary dual of an arbitrary Lie group.',r'The compact and nilpotent orbit constructions in \citet{kirillov1962unitary,kirillov1968characters} do not establish the proposed universal representation count. Equivariance, polarization, and representation-theoretic admissibility cannot be omitted.',['kirillov1962unitary','kirillov1968characters'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The line-bundle classification is a modern geometric interpretation of the integral-form and fundamental-group data, explicitly separated from the stronger representation claim.')
item(160,'P000260','theorem','The SU(2) sphere calculation and normalization',r'On a sphere of radius \(r\), \(B=(2\pi r)^{-1}d\sigma\); integrality gives \(2r=n\). The orbital transform is written \(\sin(rx)/x\).',r'With the stated normalization, \(\int_{S_r^2}B=2r\), so prequantization requires \(2r\in\mathbb Z\). For \(|X|=x\), direct integration gives \(\int_{S_r^2}e^{i\langle F,X\rangle}B(F)=2\sin(rx)/x\), with value \(2r\) at zero. Choosing \(r=(n+1)/2\) for the highest-weight-\(n\) representation and dividing by \(\sin(x/2)/(x/2)\) gives \(\sin((n+1)x/2)/\sin(x/2)\), of dimension \(n+1\).',r'This is the rank-one specialization of \citet[\S3, Theorem~2]{kirillov1968characters}. The factor two and the shift are required by evaluation at the identity.',['kirillov1968characters'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The Euclidean normalization of the Lie-algebra coordinate is fixed, and the notebook integral is completed by direct spherical integration.')
item(160,'P000260','claim','The scope of the displayed Plancherel formula',r'\(\|f\|_2^2=\int\operatorname{tr}(T(f)T(f)^*)\,d\mu(T)\) is called universal for all groups; the question is to calculate \(d\mu\).',r'For a second-countable unimodular type-I locally compact group, the Plancherel transform admits this Hilbert--Schmidt norm formula over the unitary dual, initially for a suitable dense subspace of \(L^1\cap L^2\). Unimodularity and a suitable measurable decomposition are essential to this form. Nonunimodular groups require operator-valued formal-degree corrections, and arbitrary non-type-I groups cannot be handled by the same ordinary parametrization of irreducibles.',r'For the nilpotent case this predates the talk \citep[\S7]{kirillov1962unitary}. The notebook\textquoteright s all-group formula is not established by that example or by the exponential-unimodular calculation in \citet[\S2]{kirillov1968characters}.',['kirillov1962unitary','kirillov1968characters'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The modern qualified theorem is stated; the measure-calculation programme lacks a particular new group or measure and therefore creates no separate open problem.')
item(160,'P000260','problem','Extracting the polynomial square root of a skew determinant',r'Problem: for a skew-symmetric matrix, extract a polynomial square root of its determinant; the two-by-two example is given as \(-a^2\).',r'For a skew-symmetric \(2m\times2m\) matrix over a field of characteristic different from two, \(\det A=\operatorname{Pf}(A)^2\), where \(\operatorname{Pf}(A)=(2^m m!)^{-1}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{2m}}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)\prod_{j=1}^m a_{\sigma(2j-1),\sigma(2j)}\). Equivalently, the top exterior power of its associated two-form is the Pfaffian times the coordinate volume form. Odd skew determinants vanish.',r'This is the classical Pfaffian identity underlying the top-degree form on P000258; the notebook poses an exercise, not an unsolved problem. The displayed example has determinant \(+a^2\).',[],literal='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The characteristic restriction, even size, and explicit polynomial are supplied. This elementary reconstruction is not assigned a historical problem-index identifier.')
item(160,'P000260','remark','Matrix spectra and tensor products: an unspecified duality',r'A duality is drawn between products of positive matrices (with unitary inserted) and tensor products of representations of the unitary group; Perelomov and Popov are linked to a convex region.',r'The spectra of two matrices alone do not determine the spectrum of their product without information on their relative eigenspaces. A probability distribution would additionally require a law for those eigenspaces. Tensor-product decomposition requires specified representations and multiplicity data. The notes do not distinguish an attainable-spectra problem, a random-matrix law, or a semiclassical limit, and do not define the convex region.',r'\citet[Abstract]{perelomovpopov1968casimir} is a verified contemporary paper by the named authors, but it concerns Casimir eigenvalues and is only a thematic lead, not an exact publication match to this duality.',['perelomovpopov1968casimir'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='low',reconstruction='No identification with a particular Horn problem or asymptotic tensor-product theorem is made.')
item(160,'P000260','claim','Rodrigues formulas for Legendre and Jacobi polynomials',r'\([(x^2-1)^n]^{(n)}\) is Legendre; \([(x-1)^{m_1}(x+1)^{m_2}]^{(m_3)}\) is Jacobi.',r'For \(n\ge0\), \(P_n(x)=(2^n n!)^{-1}(d/dx)^n(x^2-1)^n\). More generally, \(P_n^{(\alpha,\beta)}(x)=(-1)^n[2^n n!(1-x)^\alpha(1+x)^\beta]^{-1}(d/dx)^n[(1-x)^{n+\alpha}(1+x)^{n+\beta}]\); for the orthogonality problem one assumes \(\alpha,\beta>-1\). The raw derivative in the notebook is a weighted polynomial, not in general the normalized Jacobi polynomial itself.',r'Classical formulas, checked in the NIST \citet[\S18.5(ii), Table~18.5.1]{nist2026dlmf}; their origin predates the talk.',['nist2026dlmf'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Normalization and the Jacobi weight divisor are restored.')
item(160,'P000260','remark','Fractional Rodrigues operations and hypergeometric functions',r'When the derivative order \(m_3\) is fractional, a hypergeometric function is obtained; \(x(x-1)\) is also suggested.',r'A fractional derivative requires an integration endpoint, a branch, a kernel, and an admissible parameter domain. For one explicit choice, the Riemann--Liouville integral satisfies \(I_{0+}^{\mu}[x^{b-1}(1-x)^{-a}]=\Gamma(b)x^{b+\mu-1}\,{}_2F_1(a,b;b+\mu;x)/\Gamma(b+\mu)\) when \(\Re b,\Re\mu>0\) and \(0<x<1\). This exhibits the intended connection, but is one reconstruction, not a uniquely recoverable notebook formula.',r'The ordinary Jacobi--hypergeometric relation is classical \citep[\S18.5(iii)]{nist2026dlmf}; the fractional operation intended here is unidentified.',['nist2026dlmf'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The displayed integral identity follows by the binomial series and beta integral. It does not claim that the notebook chose this fractional-calculus convention.')
correction('P000259','Compact-orbit sentence','For an arbitrary compact group all orbits are in general position.','The rho-shifted orbits entering the irreducible character formula are regular; arbitrary coadjoint orbits need not be.','The zero orbit of a nonabelian compact group is singular. Kirillov1968 Theorem2 explicitly shifts the highest weight.')
correction('P000260','Sphere Fourier integral',r'\(\int\cdots=\sin(rx)/x\).',r'With \(B=d\sigma/(2\pi r)\), the integral is \(2\sin(rx)/x\).',r'The scan confirms the coefficient is omitted. At zero the integral must equal the symplectic area \(2r\); the notebook right-hand side tends to \(r\).')
correction('P000260','Two-by-two skew determinant',r'\(\det\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&a\\-a&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)=-a^2\).',r'\(\det\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&a\\-a&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)=a^2\).',r'The scan has a minus sign; expansion of the determinant gives \(0-(-a^2)=a^2\).')

# Record 161 is a reading programme, not a list of invented theorems.
talk(161,'1968-11-18','I. M. Gelfand','P000260-P000264','A graded reading programme in representation theory',r'The programme runs from P000261 to the upper part of P000263. Level zero covers finite and compact groups, rotations, and Lie algebras; level one proceeds through classical unitary representations, characters, induction, nilpotent groups, rank-one examples, and operator algebras. Level two adds non-Archimedean groups, symmetric-space Plancherel theory, horospheres, Borel--Weil, type-I theory, enveloping algebras, orbit methods, Kazhdan\textquoteright s work, and lattices. The closing promise of a list in Uspekhi is a publication plan, not the list itself. Individual bibliography entries without mathematical assertions are preserved here rather than inflated into occurrence items.',kind='plan')
item(161,'P000261-P000263','method','Representation theory organized by prerequisites and examples',r'Three levels are distinguished; compact-group fundamentals precede explicit noncompact examples, which precede structural harmonic analysis and enveloping algebras.',r'This is a pedagogical method: learn finite/compact representation theory and concrete rank-one models before distribution characters, induced representations, Plancherel theory, type-I decomposition, and orbit/enveloping-algebra methods. It contains no claim that a single listed text proves a complete classification for all groups.',r'The nilpotent component is concretely anchored by \citet{kirillov1962unitary}; the programme itself is source evidence about teaching and seminar priorities.',['kirillov1962unitary'],match='partial',literal='correct')
item(161,'P000261','claim','Berezin and the phrase existence of all representations',r'Berezin, Transactions of the Moscow Mathematical Society volume 6 and a letter in volume 12: description through Laplace operators, and existence of all representations proved.',r'The class of semisimple groups, the precise category of representations, the role of infinitesimal characters, and the admissible spectral parameters are not given. Existence for all algebraically imaginable Laplace eigenvalues is not a justified reading. The remark therefore cannot be converted into a precise all-group unitary-dual theorem.',r'The volume-level bibliographical lead is retained, but no theorem in the two Berezin references was read that matches the unqualified wording. This is an unresolved identification, not an open classification problem.',[],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
item(161,'P000262-P000263','remark','Enveloping algebras and the continuing orbit programme',r'Gelfand--Kirillov, the French Publications Mathématiques paper and Functional Analysis volume 3 issue 1, are listed; Kirillov and Kostant are named for general representations and characters.',r'The reference links the seminar\textquoteright s earlier study of enveloping algebras and their division rings with the orbit-method talks. A bibliography citation alone does not repeat every conjecture of the cited work, nor assert that the general orbit programme is complete.',r'The French reference identifies \citet{gelfandkirillov1966corps}; the 1969 volume reference may be prospective or a later annotation, and is not evidence that the article had already been published in November 1968.',['gelfandkirillov1966corps'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',literal='correct',reconstruction='Only the connection explicitly conveyed by the reading list is indexed; no earlier problem identifier is repeated solely because its literature is named.')
save()
print('Checkpoint',len(T),'records',len(I),'items',len(C),'corrections')

# Record 162 checkpoint.
talk(162,'1968-11-18','M. L. Gromov','P000263-P000267','Differential relations, local flexibility, and global existence',r'Gromov begins in the lower half of P000263 and continues through P000266. Jets turn PDEs into differential relations; deformation lifting replaces uniqueness, and local solvability plus suitable invariance is proposed as the route to global solutions. The precise open-manifold theorem appears in \citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable}, received 8 July 1968 and published in 1969. The closing isometric-mapping statements have illegible dimensions and missing hypotheses and are not all identified with that theorem.')
item(162,'P000263-P000264','definition','Differential relations and holonomic solutions',r'For a bundle \(\mathcal L\to M\), let \(\mathcal L^r\) be its jet extension and \(A\subset X^r\) a differential equation. The solution line writes \(\gamma\in\Gamma(\mathcal L)\), \(\operatorname{Im}\gamma\subset A\).',r'For a smooth fibre bundle \(E\to M\), an order-\(r\) differential relation is a subset \(\mathcal R\subset J^rE\). A formal solution is a section of \(\mathcal R\to M\); a genuine solution is a smooth section \(s:M\to E\) such that \(j^rs(x)\in\mathcal R\) for every \(x\). Its jet section is holonomic. A homogeneous linear differential equation of constant rank defines a vector subbundle; arbitrary equations need not be subbundles.',r'The terminology is formalized in \citet[\S4.1]{gromov1969stable}; the underlying jet construction already appears in Palamodov\textquoteright s record156.',['gromov1969stable'],reconstruction='The missing jet prolongation in the solution line is restored; regularity is supplied for the subbundle claim.',literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(162,'P000263','remark','Open and closed differential relations',r'Classical equations usually define closed submanifolds; \(Lu\ne0\) gives an open set and \(Lu=0\) a closed one.',r'If a differential operator is represented by a continuous bundle map \(L:J^rE\to F\), then \(L^{-1}(0)\) is closed and \(L^{-1}(F\setminus0)\) is open, provided the zero section is closed. Neither preimage is automatically a submanifold. Openness allows small perturbations of jets to preserve a relation.',r'The open-relation condition is an explicit hypothesis of \citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable}.',['gromov1969stable'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(162,'P000264','construction','The metric pullback equation and the immersion condition',r'\(f:(M_1,g_1)\to(M_2,g_2)\), \(f^*g_2=g_1\), as a section of \(M_1\times M_2\); add the open condition that it be an embedding.',r'The equation \(g_{2,f(x)}(df_xv,df_xw)=g_{1,x}(v,w)\) is a first-order relation. For positive-definite \(g_1\), every solution is an immersion. Full rank of \(df\) is an open first-jet condition; global injectivity and being a homeomorphism onto the image are not conditions on a single jet. Thus embedding cannot be imposed as the stated local open relation.',r'Gromov\textquoteright s published open-relation framework treats immersion rank precisely \citep[\S4.2]{gromov1969stable}; it does not identify embeddings with full-rank jets.',['gromov1969stable'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='Immersion and embedding are distinguished; the metric equation itself is a closed, not open, relation.')
item(162,'P000264','method','Parametric solvability as homotopy lifting',r'For a family \(A_N\), well-posedness means that the family of solution spaces over \(N\) is a Serre fibration; finite-parameter deformations should lift.',r'For a topological family \(\mathcal R_n\), define \(\mathcal S=\{(s,n):j^rs(M)\subset\mathcal R_n\}\). The proposed property is that \(\mathcal S\to N\) has the homotopy-lifting property for disks. The full product \(\Gamma(E)\times N\to N\) always has that property and therefore cannot encode solvability; the restricted solution space is required. This homotopical condition does not assert analytic uniqueness or quantitative continuous dependence.',r'The Serre-fibration and flexibility machinery is developed by \citet[\S\S1--3]{gromov1969stable}, circulated by its July 1968 submission and printed in 1969.',['gromov1969stable'],cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction='The total space is restricted to actual solutions; the notes use well-posedness in a homotopy-theoretic sense.')
item(162,'P000264','method','Nash inversion as the local analytic input',r'The local part of the metric example is attributed to Nash\textquoteright s implicit-function theorems; the global part is unknown.',r'For the metric-induction operator \(f\mapsto f^*g_{\mathrm{eucl}}\), local solvability near a suitably free embedding uses an inverse procedure that compensates for loss of derivatives by smoothing. A classical Banach-space implicit-function theorem cannot simply be applied when the linearized right inverse loses derivatives. A global existence principle needs further geometric hypotheses.',r'The named source is Nash\textquoteright s 1956 embedding paper \citep{nash1956embedding}; its identity and scope are confirmed in \citet[reference~3]{choquet1958nash}. The accessible Nash PDF was opened but not text-extractable, so no numbered local theorem is asserted here.',['nash1956embedding','choquet1958nash'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The free-embedding and derivative-loss conditions explain the method; they are not written out in the notebook.')
item(162,'P000265','theorem','C1 isometric approximation and the embedding distinction',r'C1-Nash: deforming the metric deforms the immersion; an immersion for one metric can be deformed for any metric, and the last line says obtain an embedding.',r'The classical Nash--Kuiper approximation theorem says that a short \(C^1\) immersion of an \(n\)-dimensional Riemannian manifold into Euclidean \(\mathbb R^q\), \(q\ge n+1\), admits arbitrarily close \(C^0\) approximations by \(C^1\) isometric immersions. With an initial embedding and the appropriate embedding version, embeddings can be retained. Deforming a mere immersion does not automatically eliminate self-intersections. The full parametric lifting statement requires its own formulation.',r'Nash (1954) and Kuiper (1955) predate this talk; their work is documented in the contemporary Bourbaki exposition \citep[title and references~1--2]{choquet1958nash}. This does not certify the notebook\textquoteright s unqualified Serre-fibration assertion.',['choquet1958nash'],literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='Shortness, positive codimension, approximation topology, and the initial embedding condition are supplied.')
item(162,'P000265','method','Local extension and microflexibility',r'The Whitney--Smale continuation principle extends a deformation from part of a region; microscopic realizability is an implicit-function theorem, and often micro implies macro.',r'For a sheaf of local solutions, microflexibility is a local short-time homotopy-extension property over compact pairs. Flexibility asks for the corresponding full homotopy lifting. Microflexibility alone is not a universal global existence theorem; the passage to flexibility uses additional invariance and geometric hypotheses.',r'\citet[\S3, especially the flexibility propositions]{gromov1969stable} gives a precise sheaf formulation of this programme; received July 1968, published 1969.',['gromov1969stable'],cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction='The two lifting properties are distinguished instead of identifying an informal micro-to-macro arrow with a theorem for all PDEs.')
item(162,'P000265-P000266','theorem','The open invariant relation theorem',r'Invariance under all diffeomorphisms is called stability; in the stable case micro implies macro, and Theorem2 gives global existence on a noncompact parallelizable connected manifold.',r'Let \(M\) be a smooth manifold without boundary and with no compact connected component. Let \(E\to M\) be a natural bundle associated to the frame bundle, and let \(\mathcal R\subset J^rE\) be an open relation invariant under the induced action of local diffeomorphisms. Then \(j^r:\operatorname{Sol}(\mathcal R)\to\Gamma(\mathcal R)\) is a weak homotopy equivalence. In particular a formal solution is sufficient for existence of a genuine solution.',r'Exact qualified publication match: \citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable}, received 8 July 1968 and published in 1969.',['gromov1969stable'],cls='A',match='exact',reconstruction='Openness, the natural bundle, formal solutions, and the meaning of open manifold are supplied from the theorem; a general stable closed relation is not included.')
item(162,'P000265-P000266','claim','Parallelizability and nonempty fibres',r'Theorem2 lists parallelizable noncompact connected \(M\), a submanifold or subbundle \(A\) with nonempty fibres, micro, and stability; a solution follows.',r'In the preceding natural-bundle setting, a chosen parallelization trivializes the relevant finite-order frame data (after the usual jet-frame splitting). If the invariant model relation is nonempty, the associated relation bundle admits a section, so Gromov\textquoteright s theorem yields a solution. Nonempty fibres of an arbitrary bundle do not by themselves give a global section; neither an arbitrary submanifold relation nor diffeomorphism invariance alone suffices.',r'This is a conditional corollary of \citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable}; the notebook abbreviations do not define a stronger general theorem.',['gromov1969stable'],cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction='The formal-section step is made explicit. The source does not justify discarding the open-relation or naturality hypotheses.')
item(162,'P000265','remark','Plateau surfaces and reparametrization invariance',r'The Plateau problem is an example of invariance under diffeomorphisms; minimal films \(\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R^3\) are mentioned.',r'The geometric minimal-surface condition is invariant under changes of source coordinates when expressed intrinsically, but a particular coordinate PDE may include a gauge. Such invariance does not establish openness, microflexibility, an unrestricted h-principle, or existence for arbitrary boundary data.',r'The additional hypotheses in \citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable} explain why the Plateau example is an invariance remark rather than an application of the open-relation theorem.',['gromov1969stable'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(162,'P000266','method','Why an open manifold has no top-dimensional handles',r'Noncompact implies there is a function without a maximum; this is attached to micro implies macro.',r'A smooth connected noncompact \(n\)-manifold admits a Morse exhaustion with no critical points of index \(n\). In the open-manifold proof, this allows the required induction along a handle decomposition of index at most \(n-1\). Merely having a function with no global maximum would be much weaker and is not the needed conclusion.',r'This is the explicit topological input in the proof of \citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable}; it is used as existing background.',['gromov1969stable'],match='exact',reconstruction='The phrase without max is interpreted as no local maxima of the Morse exhaustion, not simply unboundedness.')
item(162,'P000266','theorem','The analytic local embedding dimension',r'An \(n\)-dimensional Riemannian manifold embeds locally in \(\mathbb R^{n(n+1)/2}\), and in general a lower dimension does not suffice.',r'The Janet--Cartan local theorem applies to real-analytic positive-definite Riemannian metrics: near each point there is a real-analytic isometric embedding into \(\mathbb R^{n(n+1)/2}\). This dimension is a generic local analytic bound, not a statement that every particular metric needs it. The same assertion for arbitrary smooth metrics does not follow from the analytic theorem.',r'Janet (1926) and Cartan (1927) are identified in the contemporary reference list of \citet[references~7--8]{choquet1958nash}. The original articles were not opened, so this attribution is checked through that source.',['choquet1958nash'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='indirect',match='partial',reconstruction='Analyticity and the generic meaning of the lower-bound remark are supplied.')
item(162,'P000266','claim','Approximation by two maps with the same induced metric',r'Two maps \(f_1,f_2:M\to\mathbb R^{?s}\) are C0-approximated by smooth maps \(\varphi_1,\varphi_2\) inducing the same metric; the dimension and a formula involving \(0<\lambda<1\) are obscured.',r'A definite theorem would require the target dimension, compactness assumptions, ranks of the initial maps, allowed induced metric, regularity, and the meaning of the parameter condition. The equality \(\varphi_1^*g_{\mathrm{eucl}}=\varphi_2^*g_{\mathrm{eucl}}\) alone is clear. No unique precise quantified approximation theorem can be recovered from the surviving line.',r'The published flexibility programme \citep{gromov1969stable} is relevant, but its open invariant relation theorem does not by itself prove this closed metric equality. No exact publication match was established.',['gromov1969stable'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low')
item(162,'P000266','claim','Large-dimension approximation with homothetic induced metric',r'For large \(t\ge s\), \(t=?+An\); any map is approximated by a similar one with induced metric \(\lambda g\). The target exponent and a cancelled word are unreadable.',r'The surviving assertion suggests approximation by maps whose induced metric is a scalar multiple of a prescribed metric, but does not determine \(t\), the constant \(A\), whether \(\lambda\) is fixed or freely chosen, the regularity class, or the approximation topology. These choices change the mathematical statement.',r'Searches by Gromov, stable mappings, isometric approximation, and dimension \(s+An\) led to \citet{gromov1969stable}, but not to a theorem matching all the unreadable data. This is F, not a claim that a defined problem remains unsolved.',['gromov1969stable'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low')
correction('P000264','Definition of solution',r'\(\operatorname{Im}\gamma\subset A\), \(\gamma\in\Gamma(\mathcal L)\).',r'\(\operatorname{Im}(j^r\gamma)\subset A\subset J^r\mathcal L\).','The scan has no visible jet operator in this line, although the preceding map is explicitly written. Source and target types require the correction.')
correction('P000264','Open condition called embedding','Add the open condition that the map is an embedding.','Full rank is an open first-jet immersion condition; embedding also requires global injectivity and the correct topology.','The scan clearly says embedding. A first jet cannot distinguish an immersion with self-intersections from an embedding.')
correction('P000265','Immersion-to-embedding inference','Deform an immersion to any metric and obtain an embedding.','The immersion conclusion and the embedding-preserving approximation theorem require distinct hypotheses.','The source changes from immersion to embedding. The change is not a formal consequence of a path of metrics.')
